WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.480
Why would potassium flow into a cell, against a 28-fold concentration difference?

2
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.880
Take a cell with 140 mM K+ inside and 5 mM outside.

3
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.020
Hold it at minus −70 mV and open potassium channels.

4
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:23.760
Potassium leaves. Hold it at minus −100 and potassium comes in.

5
00:00:27.183 --> 00:00:28.943
An ion feels two pushes.

6
00:00:30.183 --> 00:00:35.043
The first is concentration: potassium drifts from where it is crowded to where it is

7
00:00:35.043 --> 00:00:37.903
scarce. The second is voltage.

8
00:00:39.203 --> 00:00:44.863
The membrane potential, is the inside minus the outside, and a negative inside pulls

9
00:00:44.863 --> 00:00:46.103
positive ions in.

10
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:49.513
Add the two pushes into one number.

11
00:00:50.893 --> 00:00:53.593
Picture the trip into the cell as a hill with two slopes.

12
00:00:54.853 --> 00:00:57.373
The concentration slope makes entering uphill.

13
00:00:58.713 --> 00:01:00.313
The voltage slope makes it downhill.

14
00:01:01.773 --> 00:01:04.633
Net flow always goes the way the total runs downhill.

15
00:01:05.717 --> 00:01:09.017
For each ion, one voltage makes the two slopes cancel.

16
00:01:10.237 --> 00:01:12.137
That is its equilibrium potential.

17
00:01:13.477 --> 00:01:19.437
For potassium, with these teaching values at 37 °C, the Nernst equation puts E_K at

18
00:01:19.437 --> 00:01:26.417
−89.1 mV. There, ions still cross, but equal numbers go each way.

19
00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.070
So compare the voltage with that green mark.

20
00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.650
At minus −70 mV, which way does potassium go?

21
00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:40.150
And at minus −100 Decide before the picture moves.

22
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.300
Minus −70 sits above the green mark.

23
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.240
The voltage slope is shallower than the concentration slope, so entering is uphill,

24
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.780
and potassium leaves.

25
00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.060
Minus −100 sits below it.

26
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.900
Entering is downhill, so potassium comes in.

27
00:02:03.883 --> 00:02:05.883
That is the rule for passive flow.

28
00:02:07.143 --> 00:02:10.743
Find the equilibrium potential, then compare Vm with it.

29
00:02:11.883 --> 00:02:15.763
For a positive ion, Vm above the mark sends net flow out.

30
00:02:17.043 --> 00:02:18.923
Below the mark, net flow goes in.

31
00:02:20.143 --> 00:02:22.063
For a negative ion, the rule flips.

32
00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:25.607
The channel did the same thing in both cases.

33
00:02:26.927 --> 00:02:30.687
It opened a path, and it chose which ion could use it, and how fast.

34
00:02:31.947 --> 00:02:33.427
The slopes chose the direction.

35
00:02:34.947 --> 00:02:38.047
Channels and passive carriers never decide which way.

36
00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.080
How does a cell move anything uphill?

37
00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.900
It couples that trip to one running further downhill.

38
00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:51.820
The sodium pump splits ATP to push 3 Na+ out and bring 2 K+ in.

39
00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:59.360
Then SGLT1 lets 2 Na+ run downhill into the cell, and that run pays for glucose to

40
00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.360
climb.

41
00:03:00.733 --> 00:03:02.933
Every crossing follows the same hill.

42
00:03:04.113 --> 00:03:07.173
A channel or passive carrier only opens the way.

43
00:03:08.413 --> 00:03:12.553
Add up the slopes of everything that moves together, and the total must run

44
00:03:12.553 --> 00:03:12.973
downhill.
